cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or data, please cite it using the metadata below. This is an exploratory research artifact; it has not been peer-reviewed."
type: software
title: "Authorization Guard Interpretability: an exploratory study of authorization reasoning, internal representation readout, and evaluation reliability in language models"
authors:
  - given-names: Parth
    family-names: Mishra
repository-code: "https://github.com/pm2m1/authorization-guard-interpretability"
url: "https://github.com/pm2m1/authorization-guard-interpretability"
abstract: >-
  Exploratory research pilot and software artifact. It evaluates Qwen3.5-9B (bf16) as an authorization guard on
  864 synthetic items from 24 scenarios with a formal three-valued oracle, probes residual-stream activations
  under scenario-disjoint validation against lexical, output-confidence and construction-aware baselines, and
  measures how escalation outcomes depend on the answer interface and scoring procedure. Activation probes did
  not show credible error-prediction value beyond item structure. Developed with AI assistance; see README.
keywords:
  - ai-safety
  - mechanistic-interpretability
  - authorization
  - linear-probing
  - llm-evaluation
  - reproducible-research
